It was necessary to create mathematical objects and ideas which
had no (or little) concrete support.  German language (and
hence German thinking) lends itself well to this.  I also
said one reason "exactly why" earlier, and that was that
it's natural for a native speaker to slam multiple words
together into one word and create a new word (hence a new
idea).  The abstract-to-concrete link is more well-travelled
in a brain that thinks in German than in some other languages.

This was just an example to show that different languages
can have different strengths.  No need to get all whiney.


